Example:

Student table:

Id    Name
101   Sarthak
102   Rahul

Marks table:

Id    Marks
101   85
102   90

Both have Id.

You want:

Id    Name      Marks
101   Sarthak   85
102   Rahul     90

That is what JoinRowSet helps you do.

How?

Student RowSet
      +
Marks RowSet
      ↓
 JoinRowSet
       ↓
Match using Id
      ↓
Combined data

JoinRowSet joinRowSet = RowSetProvider.newFactory().createJoinRowSet();
joinRowSet.addRowSet(studentRowSet, "Id");
joinRowSet.addRowSet(marksRowSet, "Id");

// No. studentRowSet is not the table name. It is the Java variable/object that represents the RowSet containing data from the Student table.

// Here "Id" is the matching column.

Then you can read the joined data:

while (joinRowSet.next()) {
    System.out.println(
        joinRowSet.getInt("Id") + " " +
        joinRowSet.getString("Name") + " " +
        joinRowSet.getInt("Marks")
    );
}